<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:ti="using:TablerIcons"
             xmlns:tile="using:ScreenShare.App.Features.Viewer.Tile.ViewModel"
             xmlns:tiles="using:ScreenShare.App.Features.Viewer.Tile.View"
             x:Class="ScreenShare.App.Features.Viewer.Tile.View.TileCard"
             x:DataType="tile:TileViewModel"
             Classes.filling="{Binding IsFullscreen}"
             DoubleTapped="OnDoubleTapped">

  <!--
    One tile, wherever it is drawn: in the grid, on the stage of the focus arrangement, in a
    window of its own, or filling a screen. One definition rather than one per host, so the menu,
    the meter and the figures cannot drift apart between them.

    The chrome is hidden until the pointer is over the tile. A grid of six tiles with six names
    and six strips of figures permanently over the pictures reads as a diagnostic screen rather
    than as something to watch.

    The stats overlay is a different thing from the chrome and not a pinned version of it: it is
    the panel a reader turns on to diagnose one stream, so it is bound to its own state and stays
    up whether or not the pointer is anywhere near.
  -->

  <UserControl.Styles>
    <!-- The chrome, revealed on hover, faded rather than snapped. -->
    <Style Selector="Border.chrome">
      <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0" />
      <Setter Property="IsHitTestVisible" Value="False" />
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource SurfaceBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource RadiusControl}" />
      <Setter Property="Padding" Value="7,4" />
      <Setter Property="Transitions">
        <Transitions>
          <DoubleTransition Property="Opacity" Duration="0:0:0.12" />
        </Transitions>
      </Setter>
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="tiles|TileCard:pointerover Border.chrome">
      <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="1" />
      <Setter Property="IsHitTestVisible" Value="True" />
    </Style>

    <!-- The frame: the tile's own shape and the fill behind a picture that has not arrived. -->
    <Style Selector="tiles|TileCard Border.frame">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource PanelBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{StaticResource RadiusTile}" />
    </Style>

    <!--
      Filling a screen, the card is the picture and nothing else. Its host letterboxes it to the
      stream's shape, so a rounded corner would cut into the frame instead of shaping a tile in a
      grid, and what is behind the picture is the black a stream sits on.
    -->
    <Style Selector="tiles|TileCard.filling Border.frame">
      <Setter Property="Background" Value="{StaticResource TileBrush}" />
      <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="0" />
    </Style>

    <!--
      Which of the two this card draws: the picture, or the plate saying the picture is in another
      window. It is stated here rather than bound on the elements because the answer is the host's
      and not the tile's - the grid's slot draws the plate while its stream is popped out, and the
      window that stream popped out into draws the picture, off the one tile both of them read.

      Clearing the source is what makes the plate free rather than merely dark. A source that is
      gone ends the subscription and hands the pool back, so a popped-out stream costs one channel
      and one set of GPU slots (Features/Viewer/Tile/View/StreamTile.cs).
    -->
    <Style Selector="tiles|TileCard tiles|StreamTile" x:DataType="tile:TileViewModel">
      <Setter Property="Source" Value="{Binding}" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="tiles|TileCard[PictureElsewhere=True] tiles|StreamTile">
      <Setter Property="Source" Value="{x:Null}" />
      <Setter Property="IsVisible" Value="False" />
    </Style>

    <Style Selector="tiles|TileCard Border.plate">
      <Setter Property="IsVisible" Value="False" />
    </Style>
    <Style Selector="tiles|TileCard[PictureElsewhere=True] Border.plate">
      <Setter Property="IsVisible" Value="True" />
    </Style>
  </UserControl.Styles>

  <!--
    Right-click is the whole control surface. One menu rather than four hover buttons, and one
    definition of it here rather than one per host: what a tile affords is the same in a grid cell
    and in a window of its own.

    Three groups, in the order a reader reaches for them: where this stream is drawn, how loud it
    is, and what is printed over it. The dividers are the grouping - a menu of seven equal rows
    makes the reader do the sorting every time it opens.

    The three arrangements carry a key each, printed at the quiet end of the row. A menu is where
    a reader finds out what a tile affords, so it is where they find out what to press instead.
    The key acts on the tile under the pointer, which is the tile they had to be pointing at to
    open this menu (Features/Viewer/Tile/View/TileKeys.cs).

    Every row here names a state rather than a transition, and shows whether that state is in
    force as a tick at the end of the row. The words and the glyph never change, so a reader
    scanning the menu is reading which of these are on rather than parsing seven verbs
    (docs/design-language.md, "Menus"). It is also what makes each row idempotent to describe:
    "Focus" is a place the tile can be in, and pressing it twice is a round trip.

    No row states a ToggleType, and that is deliberate rather than an omission. A toggling
    MenuItem writes its own IsChecked when it is clicked, which would put a second writer on
    every one of these facts and let the tick flip before the thing it describes has happened -
    Mute is a call the backend can refuse, so the row would report what the click asked for and
    then take it back. IsChecked bound one way makes the tick a read of the state and nothing
    else. The cost is that the rows do not announce themselves as checkable to a screen reader.

    The design's own themes are keyed on MenuItem and MenuFlyoutPresenter, so nothing here asks
    for a look (Design/Menus.axaml).
  -->
  <UserControl.ContextFlyout>
    <MenuFlyout>
      <MenuItem Header="Focus" Command="{Binding ToggleFocus}"
                InputGesture="{x:Static tiles:TileKeys.Focus}"
                IsChecked="{Binding IsFocused, Mode=OneWay}">
        <MenuItem.Icon>
          <ti:TablerIcon Icon="IconLayoutDistributeHorizontal" />
        </MenuItem.Icon>
      </MenuItem>

      <MenuItem Header="Own window" Command="{Binding TogglePopOut}"
                InputGesture="{x:Static tiles:TileKeys.PopOut}"
                IsChecked="{Binding IsPoppedOut, Mode=OneWay}">
        <MenuItem.Icon>
          <ti:TablerIcon Icon="IconExternalLink" />
        </MenuItem.Icon>
      </MenuItem>

      <MenuItem Header="Fullscreen" Command="{Binding ToggleFullscreen}"
                InputGesture="{x:Static tiles:TileKeys.Fullscreen}"
                IsChecked="{Binding IsFullscreen, Mode=OneWay}">
        <MenuItem.Icon>
          <ti:TablerIcon Icon="IconMaximize" />
        </MenuItem.Icon>
      </MenuItem>

      <Separator />

      <!--
        Mute and the volume are one state and sit together. Both are greyed on a stream with no
        audio track, which is a different thing from a silent one: there is nothing to be loud.
      -->
      <MenuItem Header="Mute" Command="{Binding ToggleMute}" IsEnabled="{Binding HasAudio}"
                IsChecked="{Binding Muted, Mode=OneWay}">
        <MenuItem.Icon>
          <ti:TablerIcon Icon="IconVolumeOff" />
        </MenuItem.Icon>
      </MenuItem>

      <!--
        The slider lives in the menu, which is what StaysOpenOnClick is for: without it the first
        press closes the flyout and the drag never happens. The pointer events are marked handled
        in the code-behind for the same reason - a drag that reached the menu would be read as a
        click on the item.

        It is the one row that carries a control rather than a state, so it is the one row with
        no tick: the slider's own position is the reading.
      -->
      <MenuItem StaysOpenOnClick="True" IsEnabled="{Binding HasAudio}">
        <MenuItem.Icon>
          <ti:TablerIcon Icon="IconVolume" />
        </MenuItem.Icon>
        <MenuItem.Header>
          <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*" ColumnSpacing="{StaticResource GapControl}" MinWidth="190">
            <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Text="Volume" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
            <Slider Grid.Column="1"
                    Name="VolumeSlider"
                    Minimum="0" Maximum="1"
                    Value="{Binding Volume, Mode=TwoWay}"
                    PointerPressed="OnVolumePressed"
                    VerticalAlignment="Center" />
          </Grid>
        </MenuItem.Header>
      </MenuItem>

      <Separator />

      <MenuItem Header="Stats overlay" Command="{Binding ToggleStats}"
                IsChecked="{Binding ShowStats, Mode=OneWay}">
        <MenuItem.Icon>
          <ti:TablerIcon Icon="IconChartBar" />
        </MenuItem.Icon>
      </MenuItem>
    </MenuFlyout>
  </UserControl.ContextFlyout>

  <!-- The frame's fill and shape are styled rather than set here, so the filling case can restate them. -->
  <Border Classes="frame" ClipToBounds="True">
    <Panel>

      <!--
        The picture, and where it comes from. Both are set by the styles above, because a card
        drawing the plate carries no source at all rather than a hidden one.
      -->
      <tiles:StreamTile />

      <!-- Why the tile is dark: nothing decoding, connecting, or a handle this platform cannot import. -->
      <TextBlock Classes="hint" Text="{Binding Notice}" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                 IsVisible="{Binding HasNotice}"
                 HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                 TextAlignment="Center" Margin="{StaticResource InsetSection}" />

      <!-- The plate. The slot stays in the arrangement at this stream's shape, so nothing reflows. -->
      <Border Classes="plate" Background="{StaticResource SurfaceBrush}">
        <StackPanel HorizontalAlignment="Center" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                    Spacing="{StaticResource GapTight}">
          <ti:TablerIcon Icon="IconExternalLink" Brush="{StaticResource MutedBrush}"
                         HorizontalAlignment="Center" />
          <TextBlock Classes="hint" Text="In a window of its own" TextAlignment="Center" />
        </StackPanel>
      </Border>

      <!-- The name, top left, over the picture. -->
      <Border Classes="chrome" VerticalAlignment="Top" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
              Margin="{StaticResource InsetTight}">
        <TextBlock Classes="ident" Text="{Binding Title}" />
      </Border>

      <!--
        The figures. What the pipeline turned out to be and what this window did with it - the
        chain that ran is not always the one the settings asked for, and the drop count is the
        only place a window too slow to draw becomes visible.
      -->
      <Border Classes="chrome" VerticalAlignment="Bottom" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
              Margin="7,7,7,12">
        <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Figures}">
          <ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
            <ItemsPanelTemplate>
              <WrapPanel />
            </ItemsPanelTemplate>
          </ItemsControl.ItemsPanel>
          <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
            <DataTemplate x:DataType="x:String">
              <TextBlock Classes="hint" Text="{Binding}" Margin="0,0,10,0" />
            </DataTemplate>
          </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
        </ItemsControl>
      </Border>

      <!--
        The stats overlay: the panel a reader turns on for the one stream being diagnosed.

        Bound to its own state and not to the hover chrome, which is the whole difference between
        the two. Turning it on and having it disappear when the pointer left would make it useless
        for the thing it exists for.
      -->
      <Border IsVisible="{Binding ShowStats}"
              VerticalAlignment="Top" HorizontalAlignment="Right"
              Margin="{StaticResource InsetTight}"
              Padding="{StaticResource InsetControl}"
              Background="{StaticResource SurfaceBrush}"
              BorderBrush="{StaticResource DividerBrush}"
              BorderThickness="1"
              CornerRadius="{StaticResource RadiusPanel}">
        <StackPanel Spacing="4">
          <TextBlock Classes="ident" Text="{Binding Title}" Margin="0,0,0,3" />
          <ItemsControl ItemsSource="{Binding Figures}">
            <ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
              <DataTemplate x:DataType="x:String">
                <TextBlock Classes="hint" Text="{Binding}" />
              </DataTemplate>
            </ItemsControl.ItemTemplate>
          </ItemsControl>
        </StackPanel>
      </Border>

      <!--
        The meter, along the bottom edge. Drawn whenever the decode carries audio and empty when
        it is silent; absent entirely when there is no audio track, which is the other fact.

        Not chrome, and it does not hide on hover: it is the one figure worth seeing without
        asking, and it is measured before the volume element, so a muted stream still shows itself
        making noise.
      -->
      <Border VerticalAlignment="Bottom" Height="3" IsVisible="{Binding HasAudio}"
              Background="{StaticResource DividerBrush}">
        <Border Name="Level" Height="3" HorizontalAlignment="Left"
                Background="{StaticResource ForegroundBrush}"
                IsVisible="{Binding HasLevel}" />
      </Border>

    </Panel>
  </Border>

</UserControl>
